sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/kimura-heterozygosity> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>, <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/TestableHypothesis>;
    dc:description "Under neutral mutation to new alleles and drift, expected heterozygosity reaches an equilibrium that rises with the product of effective population size and mutation rate.";
    dc:source "Motoo Kimura (1964) The number of alleles that can be maintained in a finite population";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Neutral equilibrium heterozygosity (Kimura-Crow)";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1001388>;
    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "H = \\frac{\\theta}{1+\\theta}, \\theta = 4N_e\\mu (infinite-alleles model)";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Assumptions: infinite-alleles mutation, neutrality, constant N_e, equilibrium. Formal status: proved within the model. Observed diversity varies far less across species than census sizes predict (Lewontin's paradox; Leffler et al. 2012), which points to linked selection and demography.";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/hypothesisType> "model-theorem";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/proposedTest> "Regress genome-wide neutral diversity on independent estimates of N_e times mu across species, and test whether the residual pattern is explained by linked selection."
}
